  <Icon>k__BackingField: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f303c13a2c8b41f48934b4137df4d33b, type: 3}
  <AboutText>k__BackingField: "<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>\nThe quintessential
    variant, Klondike reigns as the most popular Solitaire game in the world. It
    requires a standard 52-card deck, with 28 arranged into a 7 column staircase.\n\nThe
    game requires the player to sort all the cards into their own suit from Ace to
    King. They do this by arranging cards in alternating colour sequences until they
    can they can be moved to their final suit stacks. Whether or not a player wins
    is dependent on both their skill, and the luck of the draw.\n\n<b><size=+10>History</size></b>\nThe
    origins of Klondike are unknown, but numerous theories have been proposed. What
    can be known though, is that the game in its current form was first outlined
    in the 1907 edition of \"Hoyle's Games\" under the title of \"Seven-Card Klondike\".
    Its name was simplified to \"Klondike\" in another later book; the 1913 edition
    of \"Official Rules of Card Games\". Although Hoyle's book is the earliest written
    record of the rules, the game most likely existed for some time prior.\n\nOne
    of the major theories regarding the game's origin can be found in the Klondike
    Gold Rush (1896\u20131899). It is believed that the game was either created,
    or made widely known, during this time. Regardless of whether this is where the
    game was first devised, there is no doubt that its modern name comes from this
    event.\n\n<b><size=+10>Today</size></b>\nToday Klondike is the most popular Solitaire
    variant and is often simply referred to as Solitaire by many. The game's popularity
    is often attributed to its inclusion as a free game in early versions of Microsoft
    Windows, and today, millions of people enjoy the game in both physical and digital
    formats."
  <GuideText>k__BackingField: 
